problems.problem:
Prove that there exist:
(a) $5$ points in the plane such that among all the triangles with vertices among these points there are $8$ right-angled ones;
(b) $64$ points in the plane such that among all the triangles with vertices among these points there are at least $2005$ right-angled ones.

Let $n$ be a positive integer. Two players, Alice and Bob, play the following game:
• Alice chooses $n$ real numbers, not necessarily distinct;
• Alice writes all their pairwise sums on a sheet of paper and gives it to Bob (there are $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$ such sums, not necessarily distinct);
• Bob wins if he finds correctly the initial $n$ numbers chosen by Alice with only one guess.
Can Bob be sure to win in the following cases: (a) $n=5$; (b) $n=6$; (c) $n=8$? Justify your answers. (For example, when $n=4$, Alice may choose the numbers $1, 5, 7, 9$, which have the same pairwise sums as the numbers $2, 4, 6, 10$, and hence Bob cannot be sure to win.)

Find the maximum number $n$ of three-digit numbers such that:
1. each of them has digit sum $9$;
2. none of them contains the digit $0$;
3. any two of them have different units digits;
4. any two of them have different tens digits;
5. any two of them have different hundreds digits.

Let $k>1$ be a positive integer and $n>2018$ an odd positive integer. The non-zero rational numbers $x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n$ are not all equal and satisfy $$x_1+\frac{k}{x_2}=x_2+\frac{k}{x_3}=x_3+\frac{k}{x_4}=\cdots=x_{n-1}+\frac{k}{x_n}=x_n+\frac{k}{x_1}.$$ Find:
(a) the product $x_1x_2\cdots x_n$ as a function of $k$ and $n$;
(b) the least value of $k$ such that there exist $n, x_1, x_2,\ldots, x_n$ satisfying the given conditions.

Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer. There are $n$ coloured lamps arranged in a circle. Pressing the button of a lamp once changes its colour as follows: green $\rightarrow$ red, red $\rightarrow$ blue, blue $\rightarrow$ green. Initially, all lamps are red. Aladdin makes moves on these lamps; each move consists of the following three steps:
• he chooses a lamp $L$, without pressing its button;
• he presses the button of the clockwise neighbour of $L$ once;
• he presses the button of the counterclockwise neighbour of $L$ twice.
For each $n$, determine the maximum possible number of lamps that are green simultaneously after finitely many moves.
